    "finish_elapsed_s": 10.617716476030182,
    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant absorbs heat from your food and air, then evaporates into a gas. A compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows through a tiny valve that makes it cold and low-pressure again, ready to repeat the cycle. A thermostat switches the compressor on and off to keep the temperature steady."
  }
